namespace Patterns.Core.Services;

/// <summary>
/// The Help catalogue's long explanations — the "how it works" of every topic, one constant
/// each. <see cref="HelpTopics"/> carries the rest of a topic: where it sits in the workflow,
/// the steps, the words on the wire, the pages and the search words. Kept apart so the prose
/// can be read and edited on its own.
/// </summary>
public static class HelpBodies
{
    public const string VideoClock =
        @"Whenever a clip is on air — the program's video, a playlist's video, a stinger's clip, an audio file through the same decoder — the Show panel's PROGRESSION block, the Run strip, the phone, Companion and OSC all read one clock: the clip's name, where it is, how long it is and what is left, from the decoder itself (the length appears a moment after the clip opens). For the last ten seconds the row and the chip go red and carry the caller's word — OUT IN 7 — so 'ten seconds on VT' is read from the desk, not guessed; a clip set to loop reads LOOP with its position and never calls an out, because it never comes out. Two keys sit on the row. ⏭ LAST 10 s is the rehearsal's skip: the clip jumps to its last ten seconds and everything still happens for real — the end plays, the out is heard, the playlist moves to its next item, a stinger runs its ending — because nothing is faked; the decoder reaches the end. A timed playlist item has its own clock wound forward with it, so the running order moves when the picture does. VIDEO END 30 on the wire gives the last thirty. ⟲ RESTART plays the clip from the top (an ended clip comes back). The clip that is read is the first file the program references — its own media first, then a layer over it, then a screen of its own; a live source (a camera, NDI, a web page) has no timeline and no clock. The remotes are pushed every second while a clip runs, and only then, and only while someone is connected — a phone counts down with the desk without costing the show a frame.";

    public const string AudioPlaylist =
        @"The audio track of earlier rounds was one file with a loop — a stinger could do as much — so it became a playlist: any number of tracks added one at a time (+ FILES), and any number of folders (+ FOLDER) whose audio files play after the rows in name order, so a walk-in bed is a folder dropped on the stick and a running order of announcements is a list of rows. The order is the rows first, then each folder's files; the same file never plays twice from two places; Shuffle deals the whole order by a seed the show keeps, so a shuffled list plays the same way at the rehearsal and the show until RESHUFFLE deals again; Loop the list starts it over at its end (a single track loops seamlessly, as before), off it stops there. Every track plays to the same outputs — the computer's output, HDMI screens, a group of them, each with its own delay — locked to the master clock like the pictures. What is on reads on the Audio page and the Show panel's AUDIO block: 3/12 walk-in — 1:02 / 3:30 on 2 outputs · next: intro · shuffle · loop; ▶ NOW marks the row. ▶ PLAY starts the list where it stopped (or at the first row), ▶ on a row plays that row now, ⏭ NEXT and ⏮ PREV step it (they always wrap, so a key never dead-ends), ■ STOP stops. A file missing from the disk is skipped in the direction of travel and the running order carries on; a list with nothing on disk stops and says so. On the wire AUDIO PLAY [n|name] plays a track by its place in the order, a row's name or a file's name, AUDIO NEXT / PREV / STOP, AUDIO VOL 0–125; a cue's Play audio can name a track or leave it blank for the list, and Audio — next / previous track step it; Companion's audio keys, the phone's AUDIO tab and OSC have the same. A VOG ducks the list under it, a stinger fades it out and brings it back, the live duck holds it down, STOP ALL stops it; a show file from before the list carries its one track as the first row.";

    public const string Shell =
        @"Five groups on the rail, in the order a show happens: SHOW (the panel beside the switcher, and the Run surface) · PLAN (cues, looks and every wall-clock automation) · BUILD (pattern, media, overlays, lower thirds, countdown, particles, fractals, branding, the library) · SETUP (screens, audio, NDI, stream, remote) · ADMIN (the machine, this help). The strip across the top lists the current group's pages; each group remembers the page you were on. ? TIPS on the strip opens this page's explanations under their headings — every page keeps its own — with a tick to show them inline again (the tick above does the same; the show remembers it). On the Cues, Screens and Lower thirds pages the selected item's settings — the cue's, the screen's, the element's — pop out into a column of their own between the page and the divider, headed SELECTED CUE / SCREEN / ELEMENT in the page's colour, so the list keeps its room: ◀ CLOSE on the column hides it for that selection, SETTINGS ▸ on the page brings it back, the next selection opens it again, and it closes with the page and in RUN; ? TIPS reads its explanations after the page's. The desk resizes: drag the divider between the page and the screens, drag the handle between PROGRAM and PREVIEW, or press ◧ WIDE on the strip to reduce the screens to a strip on the right — the layout travels with the show. With the screens as a strip — ◧ WIDE on, or the Machine and Help pages, which take the room on their own — the divider sets the strip's width instead, remembered on its own beside the page's, so a drag on the Machine page never moves an ordinary page's column. SHOW CONTROLS under the wall: message, clock, countdown and the audio track's volume, each behind SEND — straight to air, sandbox open or not, journaled, and replaced by the next look recall.";

    public const string Modes =
        @"Two different things in the header. MODE — PREP or SHOW — is about what may leave the machine, and it is saved in the show file. PREP is pre-programming: every editor, the switcher, looks, cues and the clicker work exactly as in SHOW, but the output windows are refused (and closed as you enter), every NDI send stops and the stream is held — the audience sees nothing, on a cable or on the network; planned screens stand in for the rig and are adopted onto the real displays at the venue. SHOW is the venue: OUTPUTS ON opens the windows, the sends run, the stream starts when it is armed. Sound is never held by the mode — the audio track, VOGs, stingers and break music play in both, so a rehearsal at the desk can be heard. LAYOUT — RUN — is the caller's surface over either mode: the LIVE strip, the wall, the cue stack and GO take the window (POP OUT for a second monitor, /run on a tablet); Enter is GO, ↑ ↓ move standby, Esc twice is STOP ALL. It changes nothing about what goes out; leaving it is refused while the stack is armed, and switching PREP · SHOW leaves it first. The area's room is the caller's to set: the divider between the wall and the stack drags (the stack starts at about a third of the width, the show remembers it), ▸ COLLAPSE TILES turns the wall's tiles into vertical title bars — the tally and the name on their side — so the stack has the room, and a pause over any tile, collapsed or not, pops it up large with PGM and PVW side by side. One tile at a time, on either layout: ▸ at the end of a tile's title row collapses that tile alone and ▾ on its bar opens it again — the show remembers both.";

    public const string Prep =
        @"PREP in the header (or on the Screens page): build the whole rig at your desk with nothing plugged in. 'Plan a screen' adds a screen at the size the venue will have — arrange it, name it, give it its own pattern, put it in a canvas, use it in the multiview — and outputs stay held closed so nothing can go live by accident. Live inputs can be typed in by name (Media page) before the camera or NDI sender exists. At the venue, switch to SHOW, pick which detected display each planned screen turned out to be and press Adopt: position, label, rotation, trims, warp, per-screen pattern, canvas name, multiview tiles and the stream source all follow onto the real display, so the desk work is never redone.";

    public const string Inputs =
        @"Every live source runs on its own: give screen 1 a camera, screen 2 an NDI feed from the graphics PC and screen 3 a walk-in video, all at the same time. Pick the screen in the strip, then choose its source in the Media page. The same feed used in several places (two screens, the PiP inset and a multiview tile) costs one decode, not four. Multiview tiles can name their own NDI source or capture device, so a monitor wall can show four different inputs. The Media page lists what is mounted right now, and warns if the rig asks for more simultaneous sources than the limit (4 decoders, 6 NDI receivers).";

    public const string Workflow =
        @"1) SETUP › Screens — drag tiles together to span them into one canvas, click a tile to enable/disable it, rotate it for portrait mounting, trim its brightness/colour, nudge its corners (warp) for a keystoned projector, or give it its own pattern.  2) BUILD — choose and tune a pattern, or grab one from the Library (sections for patterns, your images, videos and audio, particles, presets and brand kits, and a search box); the Particles page files its scenes in packs — Classic, Awards, Modern, Nature, Moods, Starcloth, Night sky, Feel-good — with your own saved scenes under Custom; the Fractals page is the same studio for a living picture from maths — scenes by family (Mandelbrot, Julia, Burning ship, Newton, Domain warp), the view, the palette, and the sound it breathes with, this computer's or an input.  3) OUTPUTS ON.  4) PLAN › Looks — save the state as a look and put it on an F-key or the daily schedule; PLAN › Cues — build the cue stack and the clicker list.  5) SHOW — run the evening from the panel, or RUN for the caller's surface.  Everything updates live while outputs run — including LED wall geometry, blend overlap, countdowns and branding.";

    public const string FrameRate =
        @"Screens page: the master frame rate paces every output on one clock — a 30 fps show on 60 Hz displays draws every other refresh, evenly, so spans and NDI stay in step — with a per-screen override for a display that must run at its own rate; an NDI sender set to 'master' sends at it, the stream can follow it, and the Machine page judges dropped frames against it. Unlimited (the default) draws at each display's refresh. Display mode (selected screen): the sizes and refresh rates the display's EDID offers; Apply switches it through Windows while outputs are off, KEEP within 15 seconds makes it stick or it reverts by itself, and everything programmed for that screen follows it across the change. Direct output (selected screen): bypasses the desktop compositor — Windows flips the window straight to its display while a hardware card renders and the window covers the display alone, so the compositor's frame of latency and its jitter go; the swap chain behind it is chosen at start, so tick, save and restart, and the line under the tick says what is in force. A start that fails with it is held off next time; untick and tick to try again. Format (Media and PiP capture blocks): the sizes and rates the capture card's driver advertises — pick the one the source sends so the card opens in that mode; 'Device default' leaves it to the driver.";

    public const string Remote =
        @"Switch it on on the Remote page (SETUP), then open the shown address on any phone or tablet on this network for the web remote — one page with a menu across SHOW, CUES, LOOKS, SCREENS, AUDIO, LOWER THIRDS and SETUP, a header that names what is on air with its chips and a connection dot, thumb-sized buttons, and a page that changes the moment the show does; the tab you were on is remembered. Stream Decks: load the Bitfocus Companion module from integrations/companion-module-patterns (presets for transport, looks, screens, groups, presenter, stingers and audio, with live feedback) — or use Companion's Generic TCP with the one-line commands in docs/REMOTE.md. There is no password: anyone on the network can drive the show while remote control is on. OVERLAYS is the tab for the clock, the message, the countdown, the logo and the PiP: every key changes the audience's screens at once and lights from the air — the clock with its hours, seconds and date; the message's words typed into a box that follows the screen until you type, SHOW / HIDE / SCROLL; a countdown of 1, 5 or 15 minutes, any minutes (2:30, 90s) or to a time of day (19:30), with a label, counting on the phone every second and reading OVER in red; LOGO, PIP, WEATHER and ALL OVERLAYS OFF. The same verbs work from anything on the wire — CLOCK 24, MESSAGE Doors open at 7, COUNTDOWN TO 19:30, OVERLAYS OFF — and over OSC.";

    public const string Switcher =
        @"The right side works like a vision mixer. PROGRAM (top) is always what the audience sees. PREVIEW (bottom) is where you build — and it is sandboxed from the moment the app opens, so touching any editor never reaches the screens. The wall between them has a tile for every content target — PGM, every joined canvas and every stand-alone screen — with a PGM and a PVW miniature (true pictures at the target's own shape) and a tally dot: red = the audience sees it, amber = held through the next send, grey = not on air. Click a tile and the big panes take its shape and show it (and the editors work on it when it has its own pattern). OWN gives the target its own pattern instead of the program — a joined canvas can hold content of its own; MON draws the miniatures (off saves GPU on a big rig); ARM means the next CUT / TAKE changes it — un-armed, it keeps the picture the audience is seeing, and ARM ALL brings everything back; OUT on the tile's title row turns that screen's output on/off live (green while it is on); ▸ at the end of the row collapses that tile alone to a vertical title bar — the tally and the name on their side, a pause still pops it up large — and ▾ on the bar opens it again (the show remembers which tiles are bars; the Run area's ▸ COLLAPSE TILES does the whole wall at once). Groups, in both senses. Every screen is in a group by what it is for — its role: MAIN (the audience's picture), CONF (a stage monitor), INFO (a foyer or info screen), REP (a repeater of another target) — and the NDI sends and the stream are feed screens of their own (NDI, STREAM); every tile's foot line reads it (MAIN · 1920×1080, REP ↳ A · Main wall, NDI · 1920×1080), and a click on the foot line opens SETUP → Screens on that screen, where Role, whether it follows cues, and Mirror of are set — so allocating a screen to a group, or moving it, is two clicks from the switcher. The other sense is a joined canvas: on SETUP → Screens drag a screen flush against another and the two become one canvas (A, B…) with one tile here and one content target; drag it away and it is a screen of its own again; name the canvas on the Screens page. The tick on a tile, TICKED GROUPS under CUT / TAKE and FADE TO BLACK on THE TICKED GROUPS act on those canvases. Build the next look with any editor, then CUT (instant) or TAKE (crossfade) where the picker beside them says — ALL ARMED, FOCUSED (the tile you clicked, or the one you last staged on or pulled into the preview; the PGM tile means every armed screen), TICKED or TICKED GROUPS; everything outside the choice keeps its picture exactly as an un-armed tile does, and the next full TAKE lifts it — or SEND on a tile STAGES the preview's picture on that target alone: its PVW miniature shows it, the audience does not, and the tile is focused as you press so CUT or TAKE with FOCUSED puts it up there and nowhere else — a picture held on preview while you decide, the way a mixer holds one. SEND TO TICKED is the live one: it puts the preview on the ticked targets now, as their own pattern, and the preview keeps the picture for the next send. EDIT SAFE re-arms itself after a CUT or TAKE, so you are always building in safety. The other way round, → PVW on a tile loads the picture that target shows on air — its own, its source's when it repeats one, else the program's, and on the PGM tile the programme itself, which is how you get what the room is watching back into the preview to change and take again — into the preview to edit (the air is untouched; EDIT SAFE opens if it was off); the tile stays focused, so SEND stages it straight back where it came from. A picture staged on a tile the next take leaves alone is not taken: that tile keeps the picture the audience has, and the staging is dropped rather than left behind as a screen that has quietly stopped following the show. What you FIRE still goes straight to air: F-key looks, the ON AIR button on a look, scheduled cues, presenter steps, stingers and every remote command — only what you are editing is held back. Use → PVW on a look to load it into the preview instead. Blackout and OUTPUTS ON/OFF stay live through the freeze. Turn the default off in Admin → Switcher if you'd rather the preview mirrored the program. The editors always have a target: Program unless a screen with its own pattern is selected (OWN on its tile, or picked in EDITING TARGET on the Pattern page), and the EDITING banner over the page names it. The picker keeps its selection through a lock, a label, a display plugged in or a show loaded, and a screen that gives up its own pattern hands the editors back to Program — it is never blank.";

    public const string ShowPanel =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: everything built on the PLAN and BUILD pages is run from here, beside the switcher, during the show — one page the operator never has to leave. Top to bottom: CUES is the caller's stack in a strip — the cue on STANDBY with its planned and expected time, the NEXT cue after it, GO (the standby cue fires and the next one moves up), HOLD (nothing fires until it is released), ARM (the stack disarmed cannot GO — the app always opens disarmed), the ▲ ▼ that move the standby cue and a CANCEL for an auto-follow that is counting down; the Run page (SHOW → Run) is the same stack full screen for a caller with a metre to read from. LOOKS are one press each — the tile goes red while its picture is on air and green while it is loaded in the preview; PVW on a tile loads it into the sandboxed preview instead of sending it, for a sign-off before TAKE; F1–F12 recall them from anywhere. SCREENS — EACH ON ITS OWN is a row per screen and canvas: pick a look in the row and press → THIS SCREEN and that look's picture for that screen (its own picture in the look if it had one, else the look's program) lands on that screen alone as its own pattern — every other screen stays exactly as it was; PROGRAM puts the screen back on the program; LOCK keeps its picture through looks, cues, TAKE ALL and stingers (a confidence or info screen); ON/OFF is the screen's output. A screen's own send is live, journaled, and survives TAKE; a whole-look recall or a cue replaces it (unless the screen is locked). PROGRESSION is where the show is going next: the clicker list's place and its NEXT / BACK (Page Down / Page Up from a USB clicker, on when Clicker armed is ticked), a deck's page, an auto-follow counting down, and the playlist's part — the three ways a show moves by itself or by a click, read in one line. Then the VOGs, stingers, lower thirds and people, the audio track, break music, FREEZE / FADE / LOOK BACK and REVIEW as before. On the wire the per-screen sends are SCREEN <n> LOOK <name> and SCREEN <n> PROGRAM, a cue's Screen — its own look / Screen — back to the program, /patterns/screen/<n>/look over OSC, Companion 2.3.0's screen_look and screen_program keys. The VOG, STINGER, LOWER THIRD and PEOPLE chips sit three to a row: a name over a small line that says what the chip is (the kind, the after-choice, the person a design holds, the role) until it goes live — then the border lights red on air or green in the preview and the line reads the tally (ON AIR · 12 s, HOLDING, ARRIVING, IN PREVIEW; for a person, ON AIR · Neon, the design that carries the name), so what is live reads from across the desk and the words are there up close.";

    public const string Installs =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a shop window, a hotel lobby, a museum wall, a reception screen — a machine nobody sits at that must show the right thing at the right time for months, take an advert or an announcement in its stride, be looked after from somewhere else and take a new build without a visit. PLAN → Install is its home. Build the looks first (Looks page), then the rota: + PROGRAMME is a look from a start to an end on its days (blank = every day; Mon–Fri, weekends, Sat Sun) between its dates (a seasonal row beats the daily one; a window past midnight is allowed); + ADVERT is a look for some seconds at its start and every so many minutes until its end — on every screen, or only the screens it names, the others keeping their picture; + ANNOUNCEMENT is words on the message overlay, a VOG from the Audio page, a look of its own, for some seconds — by the clock or by hand. TODAY shows the day as the clock will run it and says in words what a row cannot do. Tick Schedule on: the programme's look lands at once and every change goes through the same action layer as the desk, so the journal reads ApplyLook from schedule; outside every programme the idle look or black. Announcements beat adverts; a firing while the caller's stack is armed or a stinger holds the screens is skipped and said. By hand from anywhere: PLAY NOW on a row, ANNOUNCE Closing time or ANNOUNCE some words and ADVERT Offer on the wire (ANNOUNCE OFF / ADVERT OFF end them), a cue's Announcement on / Advert — play now, /patterns/announce over OSC, Companion 2.2.0's announce and advert keys, the phone's ADMIN page. Remote administration: type an admin passcode and the web remote gains an ADMIN page (health, the schedule, every announcement and advert as a key, RESTART, the staged update, a support bundle to download, the log, a console) — the same passcode rides RESTART and UPDATE APPLY on the wire; SUPPORT BUNDLE writes a zip with the logs, the journal, the settings with every secret blanked and the last super-check to send with a question. A management server: type its check-in URL (https, or http on the LAN) and a token, and the site posts its name, build, health and STATE every few minutes, running the reply's commands and staging the update it offers. Updates: drop patterns-update-<version>.zip (Patterns.exe and patterns.update.json at its root) into the updates folder beside the settings, press APPLY (or let the update window do it at a quiet hour): the watchdog swaps the files between two starts with the old ones kept in a backup folder, brings the show back live, and rolls back by itself if the new build does not stay up for two minutes. docs/INSTALLS.md carries every rule and the check-in contract.";

    public const string Interactive =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a physical GO button for the caller, a lamp on the lectern that says the deck is live, a relay that fires with a look, a sensor that changes the message — the room wired to the show. SETUP → Interactive is the page: switch the area on (off by default; a port opens only when you say so), + ARDUINO (SERIAL) or + DEVICE OVER IP, a name, the port (the page lists this machine's serial ports) or the address, the baud or the IP port, the line ending the device expects. The device speaks short text lines and Patterns turns each one into a show command through the same action layer as the desk, the cues, the phone and Companion: a trigger row maps the device's line (BTN1, whole and case-blind; SENSOR * as a prefix) to a protocol line (CUE GO, NEXT, LOOK Walk-in, BLACKOUT ON, MESSAGE Room at * with the sensor's value carried through), and a line with no row runs as the protocol it already is when you allow it. Every command is fenced like any other — a GO from a button is refused while the stack is not armed — and the answer, OK or ERR and the reason, goes back to the device; the journal reads 'from device Arduino'. Patterns speaks back too: KEY VALUE lines only when a value changes — BLACKOUT 1, LIVE 1, LOOK Walk-in, PROGRAM …, CUE 01.020, ARMED 1, HOLD 0, DECK 3 12, STEP 2 5 — everything once when a device connects and again with RESEND ALL, so a sketch follows the show in a few lines. Sending a line of your own: the SEND box on the card (a handshake to try the wiring), a cue's Device — send a line (target the device, value the text — the checks refuse an unknown device and warn while the area is off), DEVICE Arduino RELAY 1 on the wire, /patterns/device/Arduino over OSC, Companion 2.1.0's device_send. The wires look after themselves: a serial port that vanishes reopens when it is back, a TCP link reconnects every two seconds, UDP needs no connection; the card reads open, reconnecting or closed and why, with the last line in and out, so a wiring fault shows before doors. docs/ARDUINO.md carries the sketch (two buttons, two LEDs), a Raspberry Pi script and the whole vocabulary both ways.";

    public const string CompanionBanks =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: the Stream Deck at front of house should be ready the moment the show is — and stay right as looks are added, renamed and reordered in the last hour. Companion cannot place keys on a page by itself, so Patterns and its module (2.0.0, integrations/companion-module-patterns) do the next best thing: banks. Drag a row of Look bank keys once and each key reads its name from a variable Patterns keeps fresh in every STATE push ($(patterns:look_3) is the third look on the Looks page), fires the look at that place (LOOK #3 on the wire — the third look whatever its name or F-key), turns green while that look is on air and amber while it is loaded in the preview, and dims while nothing sits behind it; save a look and the next dim key lights with its name, delete one and its key dims. The same banks exist for the F-keys, lower thirds, people, VOGs and stingers, break music, playlist parts, screens (with a lock key each, green while enabled, amber while locked) and the cue stack — the Cue bank's first key is the standby cue and the six after it the cues that follow, each relabelling on every GO, a press putting that cue on standby (or standby and GO). The module also builds a preset per item — Looks — this show, People — this show, Upcoming cues — this show and the rest — rebuilt the moment the show's lists change, so a key with a fixed name is one drag away. What comes back: every key's light comes from the STATE Patterns pushes (airLook, previewLook, pattern, the looks by place with their F-key, on-air and in-preview flags, the screens with armed and own, the deck's page, the web page's service) — the feedbacks look_on_air, look_bank_on_air, look_f_on_air, look_preview, screen_armed, screen_own and slot_empty, the variables air_look, preview_look, pattern and the banks. OSC gets the same facts as messages to the feedback host — /patterns/state/looks/3 ""Walk-in"" and /looks/3/air 1, /lowerthirds/n, /people/n, /stingers/n, /sections/n, /music/items/n, /screen/n/name, /cue/next/k, /deck/page, /deck/count, /deck/ended, /web/page, /web/service, /look/air, /look/preview, /pattern — and /patterns/look/index/3 applies a look by place, so a TouchOSC page or a lighting desk labels itself the same way (Remote page → OSC). Everything a key does still goes through the one action layer: the same checks, the same journal line with the key's name, the same ERR sentence back. Module 2.5.0: the connection's settings have a checkbox per group of presets — Transport, Cue stack, All looks, All patterns, Clock functions, Countdown functions, Message and ticker, Overlays, All VOGs, All stingers, All lower thirds, All people, Screens, Audio, Presenter, Install — and only the ticked groups reach the preset list, so a desk that runs looks, stingers and lower thirds ticks those and sees nothing else (the actions and feedbacks stay complete). The Clock, Countdown, Message and Overlays groups drive the overlays from keys: CLOCK reads the time and toggles, 12 H / 24 H, SECONDS, DATE; the countdown key reads what is left — green while it runs, red when over — with 1 / 5 / 10 / 15 / 30 MIN, TO a time, a LABEL and STOP; MSG reads the words, SAY puts edited words on, SCROLL makes a ticker; LOGO, PIP, WEATHER and OVERLAYS OFF; and Patterns — every kind is a key per kind of picture (PATTERN <kind> on the wire), lit while it is on air.";

    public const string EdgeBlend =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a wide stage gets three or four projectors in a row, a tall one a stack, a big surface a 2×2 grid, and the question is always whether the blends stay independent and still add up. They do. Every output fades its own edges from its own overlaps on the Screens page: a middle projector fades both sides, a grid's projector a side and a top or bottom, each by exactly the overlap it shares with that neighbour (unequal overlaps stay on their own joins), with its own curve, its own gamma (projectors differ) and, when Automatic is off, its own typed widths. Where two zones cross in a corner the fades multiply — which is what four projectors sharing a corner need, since (left + right) × (top + bottom) is one — so the corner reads as flat as the rest. Setting it up: tick Automatic on every projector, drag each over its neighbour by the overlap (the overlaps join them into one canvas that all of them draw), select each projector in turn and read the lines under Edge blend: ✓ Left join with P1: 320 px, both fade, SCurve is a join that is right; ⚠ names what is wrong and what to do — an overlap nobody fades (twice as bright), a join fading on one side only (tick Automatic on the other), widths that differ (use the overlap on both), curves that differ (use the same curve), or zones too wide to leave a picture between them. Then put the Projection blend pattern on the canvas — Projectors along, Rows across for a grid, the real overlaps both ways — and its P1…Pn frames, hatched zones with OVERLAP n px and opposing ramps land on the wall; the 50% grey check shows every join and every corner, and each projector's Blend gamma is turned until its joins read flat. Only the outputs fade: the PREVIEW pane, the multiview, NDI sends and the stream show the canvas whole. Limits, honestly: the blend is a soft edge, not a full warp engine (the corner nudges handle a light keystone), and black levels in the overlaps depend on the projectors' native black — a projector with lifted blacks shows the zones on a black picture, which no software can remove.";

    public const string PowerPoint =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a speaker hands over a .pptx an hour before doors and nobody wants to open PowerPoint on the show machine. Choose the file as the Deck (Media page → Deck → Browse…; .pptx, .ppt, .ppsx, .pptm, a Keynote .key or an Impress .odp) and Patterns converts it to a PDF with LibreOffice Impress in the background — headless, with a profile of its own, so a LibreOffice or PowerPoint window open on the machine is neither used nor disturbed — and keeps the PDF under decks/ beside the show, named for the file's size and last write: an unchanged deck never converts twice (a look recalls it at once, even after a restart) and an edited one converts again by itself; RELOAD forces it. While it converts the card reads Converting… (the readout under the file box, the phone's deck line and STATE's converting flag say the same; the click-through steps the clicker list meanwhile) and the moment the pages arrive the deck is a PDF deck like any other: full frame at the slides' shape, sharp on every output, the click-through, the cue stack resuming at the end. Where LibreOffice comes from: install it free from libreoffice.org (the default folder is found), put a LibreOfficePortable folder beside Patterns on the show drive, or type the path to soffice.exe (or its folder) in the box that appears when it is nowhere — kept in Admin, found without a restart. Without it the card says so and what to do, and exporting the deck as PDF from PowerPoint is always the fallback. Known limits, honestly: a slide becomes a page, so animations, builds and transitions inside the PowerPoint are not kept (a build that reveals bullets becomes its final state; split it into slides if the reveal matters), embedded video does not play (put the clip on a layer or a stinger), and a font the machine lacks is substituted — check the pages on the PREVIEW pane once before the show. A 300-page deck with pictures converts in well under a minute; a conversion that takes over three minutes is stopped and the card says so.";

    public const string PdfDecks =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: most presentations arrive as a PDF — exported from PowerPoint, Keynote or Slides — and a show wants them on screen exactly as designed, driven by the presenter's clicker, and handed back to the cue stack when they end. A deck is a Media pattern (Media page → Deck): choose the PDF, and the page on show is a picture like any other — full frame at its own shape (a 4:3 deck gets bars on a 16:9 screen, never a stretch; Fill or the area of interest change that when you want), rendered as sharp as the largest screen needs on every output, NDI send and the stream, and carried by looks and cues. The pages either side are rendered ahead, so a turn is instant. The click-through: once the deck is on air — through a cue, a look, or by hand — the presenter's clicker (Page Down / Page Up, the arrows outside a text box), NEXT / BACK on the phone, Companion's presenter keys and NEXT on the wire turn its pages first, before they step a clicker list, and a list need not be armed at all. At the last page the next click GOes the caller's standby cue — the cue stack resumes, so the caller programmes 'deck on' as one cue and 'what comes after' as the next, arms the stack with the second on standby and lets the presenter click to the end; the PREVIEW header and the phone say when that click is coming, and Companion's DECK key turns amber. Untick 'At the last page, the next click GOes…' for a deck that should hold its last page instead. Start at page sets where a look or a cue lands. Turning pages from the desk: the DECK block's First / Previous / NEXT PAGE / Last, a cue (Deck — next page, previous page, go to page: a number, first or last), DECK NEXT / PREV / FIRST / LAST / PAGE n on the wire, /patterns/deck/… over OSC, and Companion 1.11.0's deck keys; STATE carries deck{file,page,count,ended}. The same deck on two screens is one deck on one page, and the preview shows the page on air. PDFs join the Library as decks, so a tile puts one on the pattern; a PowerPoint file itself goes through LibreOffice Impress — the section above.";

    public const string WebPages =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a web page is a picture like any other — the pattern (Media → Web page, or SHOW THIS PAGE ON THE PATTERN on the Remote & web page) or a layer over one (the Layers page → Web page) — so it fits the canvas, joins spans and trims, reaches NDI and the stream, crossfades, sits under the overlays and the lower third and travels with looks and cues; nothing ever opens outside Patterns, and a link that wants a new window opens in the same page. The page renders through WebView2, the browser engine Windows 10 and 11 ship, in a window of its own that no screen shows, and every click, drag, wheel step and key the desk sends reaches it the way a browser automation session sends them — as real, trusted events, whichever window has the focus — so links, players, sliders and frames from other sites all answer. Driving it: click, drag and scroll on the PREVIEW pane (Alt-press drags a web layer's box instead); PAGE CONTROLS on the Media page type into the field you last clicked and carry the page's own buttons — on YouTube PLAY / PAUSE, MUTE, RESTART, +10 s and −10 s through the player itself; on Google Slides and PowerPoint for the web NEXT, PREVIOUS, FIRST, LAST, PRESENT, BLACK, WHITE and EXIT; on any page the arrows, the page keys, Enter and Escape. KEYS → PAGE (the chip on the PREVIEW pane's header and under PAGE CONTROLS, or Ctrl+Alt+K) hands the page the whole keyboard — F5 starts a PowerPoint, the arrows move a deck, a search box takes typing, a sign-in takes a password — and the desk's own shortcuts wait until the chip or Ctrl+Alt+K gives them back. The services: paste a YouTube, Vimeo or Google Slides link and the Media page names the service (Treat it as — Auto reads the address, and naming it outright is for an address that does not say: a short link, a corporate proxy, an embed on the client's own hostname) and offers FULL FRAME, which rewrites the address so the show sees the player or the deck alone — YouTube with autoplay and no controls or related videos, a published Slides deck as its embed with the control bar hidden, your own deck in present mode — and SHOW THIS PAGE ON THE PATTERN does that by itself; a video whose owner blocks embedding keeps its watch link and the area of interest cuts the furniture. CLEAN (a tick, off until you ask for it) takes off what an address cannot: on YouTube the media bar, the channel watermark, the pause panel, the end-screen cards and the big centre play button; on Vimeo the control bar, the title and the outro; on Slides the viewer's toolbar; on any other page the margin, the scrollbar and the pointer. It is a style sheet put into the page before the page's own scripts run, so a player that rebuilds its controls never gets to draw them — and it is the site's own class names, so a service that renames one lets a piece of furniture back rather than breaking the page. It is one picture: what the room sees the operator sees, so a clean page is driven from the desk instead — PAGE CONTROLS, a cue, the phone, a Stream Deck — and a player answers whether or not its own buttons are drawn. PowerPoint for the web and a Slides deck of your own need a sign-in once — KEYS → PAGE, type, and the account stays signed in on this machine. Cues: 'Web page — key or action' with the page left blank reaches the page on air with next, prev, first, last, present, exit, play, pause, mute, restart, black, white or a key chord (ArrowRight, Space, k, Ctrl+Shift+F5), so a caller's sheet reads 'page: present' on the cue that starts the deck and 'page: next' on every slide; a page named by its nickname or a word of its address reaches that page instead; 'Web page — click' clicks a spot in percent, 'type' types, 'reload' reloads, and the checks refuse a key nobody knows or a page that will not be on air. The same on the wire (WEB NEXT, WEB KEY ArrowRight, WEB CLICK 50 50, WEB TYPE …, WEB RELOAD, WEB OPEN address, PAGE as an alias), over OSC (/patterns/web/next …), on the phone's SHOW tab (PAGE ON AIR with the service's buttons) and in Companion 1.10.0 (a Web page category, a page-on-air feedback, the page's name, title and service as variables). Set the page size (the viewport it lays itself out for) and the zoom on the Media page; 'Show the pointer' draws the desk's pointer and a click ripple on the page wherever it is shown, so the room sees what you point at — off for a page nobody drives. The same address on several targets costs one browser; four pages run at once.";

    public const string Crop =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a live input rarely arrives clean — a Teams caller comes with the gallery strip and the toolbar, a Google Slides window with its tab bar, a shared screen with a taskbar, a camera on a capture card the wrong way up — and the audience should see the picture, not the furniture around it. The area of interest is the part of an input the show uses: it is cut from the source before the picture is fitted to the target, so what is left fills the screen at its own shape on every output, every NDI send and the stream, and it is saved with the pattern, so a look or a cue that recalls the input recalls the crop. Media page → AREA OF INTEREST: press PICK ON PREVIEW and drag a box on the PREVIEW pane around what should be seen; the box is the picture the moment you let go, and the summary line says what is kept (Keeps 50% × 50% of the picture, from 25% in, 25% down). Pick again to refine: a second box is a box within the picture as it shows now, so you can close in twice. The presets take what usually needs taking — Top bar off (8%), Side panel off (25% from the right: a participants or chat panel), Bottom strip off (12%: a toolbar or notes), Centre 80% — and the four sliders cut up to 90% from any side while never keeping less than a twentieth; Clear brings the whole picture back. Mirror and Upside down flip the picture, Turn stands it at 90°, 180° or 270° for a portrait feed or a camera mounted on its side; the flips happen first, the turn after. It works on every source — a still, a video, an NDI feed, a capture card, a web page and each playlist item — through the source's own crop where it has one, so a 4K feed cropped to a quarter costs no more than the quarter. A web page keeps its clicks through the crop while it is upright (the desk maps a click on the PREVIEW pane back to the page through the box); a turned or mirrored page shows no pointer and takes no clicks — crop it, do not turn it, when you need to drive it. The PiP inset has its own four crop sliders on the PiP block for the same reason.";

    public const string MultiviewTally =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: the multiview is the monitor wall the show caller and the director watch — a screen of its own (the Pattern page's Multiview pattern on any display), an NDI send of it, or /multiview in a browser — and a monitor wall has to say more than what a picture looks like: it has to say what state each screen is in. So every tile wears badges along its top edge. PGM with a red border while the target is live to the audience (the outputs open, the screen switched on, no blackout); OFF when the screen is switched off, OUTPUTS OFF when the outputs are closed, BLACK during a blackout, FROZEN in cyan while FREEZE holds the outputs. With EDIT SAFE open — when there is a preview and a TAKE to come — NEXT in green means the next TAKE changes this target and HELD in amber means the wall un-armed it, so the caller can see at a glance where the next picture lands before pressing TAKE. LOCKED means the screen keeps its picture through looks, cues and TAKE ALL; OWN means it shows a picture of its own rather than the program; REP n means it repeats screen n. The Preview tile is green-bordered PVW (NO PREVIEW while EDIT SAFE is off). Under each tile a two-part bar: the name on the left and what it is on the right — SCREEN 2 · 1920×1080, CANVAS A · 3840×1080 · 2 SCREENS, NDI SEND 4 · 1920×1080, STREAM, PLANNED SCREEN, with CONF / INFO / REP for a screen with a role — so a tile is never a mystery picture; the PROGRAM tile's bar lists the screens the program is on (ON 1 · 2 · A) and the PREVIEW tile's the screens the next TAKE reaches (NEXT TAKE → A). The tally checkbox on the Pattern page switches the borders and badges off together; the captions stay. The same facts reach the remotes: STATE carries editSafe and each screen's armed and own, OSC feedback sends /patterns/state/editsafe and /armed/n, and the phone's SCREENS tab reads NEXT / HELD / OWN beside each screen.";

    public const string LowerThirdsFlow =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a lower third rides over whatever picture is on — a look, a cue, a clip — on every screen, NDI send and the stream at once, so it is programmed beside the picture, not inside it. The flow on the Lower thirds page: build a design (or pick the show's style once and ★ it as the default), put a person in from the LIBRARY, press PVW — the design shows on the PREVIEW pane, on the multiview's Preview tile and, with REVIEW on, full-frame on the multiview, where a show caller or director reads the spelling and signs it off while the audience sees nothing new — then TAKE TO AIR: it arrives the way it was designed to and the preview clears for the next name. AIR skips the sign-off and puts the design on straight away, as it is at that moment. HIDE takes it off the way it was designed to. The preview needs EDIT SAFE (with it off, the preview is the air, and PVW says so). What EDIT SAFE means for a design on air: the audience sees a copy, and the desk edits the show's own — so a change made while a design is on air lights EDITED on the page, the Show panel, the phone and a Stream Deck key, and UPDATE ON AIR carries it across in place, the words too, without the design leaving and arriving again (AIR again restarts it instead). A picture TAKE keeps whatever lower third is on air exactly where it is in its life and takes one that is in the preview to air with it; switching EDIT SAFE off keeps it too. The ★ default: once a style is chosen it usually serves the whole show, so PERSON n, the PEOPLE chips, the phone's PEOPLE buttons and a cue that names a person but no design put the next name into the design on air — or, with none on air, the ★ one. The first design of a show is the default until another is starred; a deleted default moves to the first design left. Cues: Lower third on / to preview / take / off — a caller's sheet can read 'lt preview: Jane' a minute before and 'lt take' on the cue. The wire and OSC: LT n, LT n WITH person, LT PREVIEW n [WITH person], LT PREVIEW WITH person, LT TAKE, LT UPDATE, LT PREVIEW OFF, LT OFF, PERSON n; STATE carries lowerThird, lowerThirdPreview, lowerThirdDefault and lowerThirdEdited, and Companion 1.9.0 has the keys (LT PVW n, PVW PERSON n, LT TAKE lit amber while something is in the preview, LT UPDATE lit while the air copy is behind). The phone's LOWER THIRDS tab has PVW FIRST, so its chips go to the preview instead of air, a TAKE TO AIR button while something is in the preview, and UPDATE ON AIR while the air copy is behind. The panel's LOWER THIRDS and PEOPLE chips sit three to a row, each a name over a small line: the person a design holds, or a person's role, until it goes live — then the border lights red on air or green in the preview and the line reads the tally (ARRIVING, ON AIR, LEAVING, IN PREVIEW; for a person, ON AIR · Neon, the design that carries the name). A person lights whichever way the name reached the design — a chip, a cue, the wire, Companion — and a name typed over by hand lights nobody.";

    public const string AudioMonitor =
        @"Audio page → WHAT THE DESK IS LISTENING TO. A show can have a clip on the programme, another on a confidence screen's own picture and a third loaded into the preview, and every one of them has a soundtrack. Nothing was choosing between them, so they played together: a mix nobody asked for, over the top of whatever the operator was actually trying to hear — and the more carefully a show is built the worse it gets, because the busier the rig the more clips are mounted at once. With EDIT SAFE open it happened on every show, since the picture being built is nearly always mounted beside the one on air.

So the desk monitors one picture at a time. Unless you say otherwise it is the programme, because the sound the room is hearing is the sound you should be checking against. The other choices: the preview, so the next clip can be heard before it lands; one output on its own, named beside the picker — a confidence screen with its own clip, a canvas, an NDI send or the stream, so a screen that is doing its own thing can be listened to without taking the programme down; or nothing, which leaves every clip silent at the desk.

What it does not do matters as much. It changes nothing on any screen, any NDI send or the stream: what the audience hears comes off the picture that is on air, never off the desk's own speakers, so a clip silent here is still heard in the room. It is not part of what a picture looks like either — choosing a monitor never crossfades a screen — and it is never carried by a look or a cue. And it only ever takes sound away: a clip you have muted on the Media page stays muted whatever you are listening to.

Picking an output that is simply following the show gives you the programme rather than silence, because that is genuinely what that screen sounds like. What is monitored is the media — clips, capture boxes and web pages. The audio playlist, VOGs, stingers and the tone generator are the show's own sound and always play.";

    public const string MultiviewWalls =
        @"A monitor wall is the show's, not one screen's. SETUP → Multiview holds up to two of them; each has its own arrangement, its own tiles and its own set of outputs, and each is one configuration however many outputs it is on — so two screens showing the same wall are showing the same wall rather than two copies that drift apart as the night goes on.

Where it was, and why it moved. The multiview used to be a pattern type, which put it in the one place it could not usefully be: to build a wall you had to make it the picture of whatever you were editing, so the programme could not be a pattern and a wall at the same time, and a wall on a second screen was a second set of tiles to keep level by hand. Now a pattern with the Multiview type only says which wall it draws, and the walls live on their own page. A show made before this keeps exactly what it had: its tiles become a wall the show holds, laid out as the even grid the old build drew, and every target that was showing them points at it.

The arrangement. Programme and preview large with the rest beneath is the default, because a wall is read at a glance from across a room and the two pictures that decide anything are what is on air and what is going on air next; an even grid says a foyer feed matters as much as the programme, which is never true. The others: one large with the rest beneath (a confidence monitor with the rig to glance at), one large down the left with the rest in a column on the right (the shape a tall monitor beside a desk wants), and the even grid for a rig check or a wall of inputs. Which tiles are the large ones is simply which are first in the list, so dragging a tile to the top with its grip (⠿) is how you say ""that is the one I am watching"" — no second setting, and what you are looking at while you drag is the finished wall. Small tiles across sets the strip; on the even grid it sets the whole wall. Labels and the tally can each be switched off.

The tiles. Programme, the preview (what the desk is building — a slate while EDIT SAFE is off), any screen or joined canvas, an NDI feed, a capture device, the PiP input, or a clock. Every one is a true miniature of what that target really shows, at its real shape — a 3840×1080 canvas is a wide strip and a portrait screen a tall box, never a re-layout at 16:9 — with the tally on it: a red border and PGM live to the audience (OFF, OUTPUTS OFF or BLACK when it is not, FROZEN while the outputs hold), green PVW for the preview, NEXT in green when the next TAKE changes it, HELD when it is un-armed, LOCKED, OWN for a screen on its own picture, REP for a repeater. Under each: its name, then which output it is — SCREEN 2 · 1920×1080, CANVAS A · 3840×1080 · 2 SCREENS, NDI SEND, STREAM.

Where it shows. Tick an output under WHERE IT SHOWS and it draws this wall instead of the programme: a screen on the rig, any NDI sender, or the stream. Ticking a sender or the stream points that feed at its own picture as part of the same tick, so there is nothing left to set on the NDI or Stream page afterwards; unticking hands the output straight back to the programme, and removing a wall hands back every output it was on. The line beside each output says what it is doing instead — showing the programme, showing the other wall, or on its own picture — so nothing is taken over by surprise. Watch a wall remotely at the web remote address + /multiview (add ?n=2 for the second).

REVIEW puts the sandboxed preview over the whole of every multiview with a chip that says so, until you switch it off: a sign-off step between EDIT SAFE and TAKE, checked on the monitor wall before it reaches the room. It is never saved with the show.";

    public const string Transitions =
        @"How one picture becomes the next, on SETUP → Screens → TRANSITIONS. Every recall the desk makes — a look, a cue, a TAKE, a playlist advance, a source change, a screen going black on its own — runs the same change, synchronised across every span, every NDI send, the stream and the desk's own miniatures, over the seconds set beside the tick. HOW chooses the shape of it. Dissolve is what a desk has always done and what a show that says nothing else gets: the outgoing picture fades away over the incoming one. Dip goes out through a colour and back with the change made underneath it — the broadcast standard, and the safest thing to put between two pictures that have nothing in common; it takes the brand's background colour unless you untick and name one. Wipe travels a soft edge across and changes the picture behind it, Travels says which way and Edge how soft (0 is a hard line, wide is a slow bleed). Push brings the new picture in from the far side as the old one leaves, the two moving together so it reads as one movement. Brand stinger is the show's own identity swept over the cut: two bars in the brand kit's primary and secondary come in from the sides, the background fills behind them, the logo lands at the peak and the picture changes underneath — a stinger with no clip to prepare, no file to lose on the stick and nothing to re-render when the client changes their colours; set them on BUILD → Branding and every stinger follows. Reactive uses a reactive scene's own picture as the matte — the change lands where the scene is dark first, so a plasma dissolves in clouds, a vortex spirals in, a kaleidoscope folds and a star warp opens from the middle; Scene chooses which.

Why they are all safe to use, at every point of a show: not one of them needs a graphics card. Three of this desk's sinks have one and three do not — NDI, the stream and the preset thumbnails draw on the processor — so a transition that needed a shader would show the client on the stream something different from the room. Every kind here is plain canvas work: a rectangle, a gradient, a translate, or one small bitmap used as a mask. The reactive matte is drawn once at 256 pixels wide when the change starts and scaled up, so a 4K wall and a thumbnail cost the same and a transition allocates nothing per frame; the buffer is let go the moment the change is over. Everything the transition needs — the kind, the way, the scene, the colours — is settled once at the instant it arms, so a setting changed while a wipe is halfway across the screen cannot turn it into a push in front of the room, and a change that throws for any reason is dropped and the picture the show is meant to be showing is drawn instead.

A dip through a bright colour is a whole-screen light change, and it goes past the same three-a-second limit a strobing sting goes past: too soon after the last one and that one change is drawn as a dissolve instead. The picture still changes; it just does not flash to do it.

One recall can carry its own arrival, for that recall alone. In a cue's look action (or LOOK on the wire), the transition box takes ""cut"", a fade in milliseconds, or a transition by name in any order: wipe 800, dip, stinger, wipe left 600, reactive vortex 1200, 1200 reactive, push up. Naming one is asking for a transition, so it happens on a show whose crossfades are switched off, and the change after it is the show's own again. The cue sheet prints what you wrote — Look ""Reveal"" (wipe right to left, 600 ms) — and the checks refuse a word the desk cannot read before the show rather than guessing at it in front of the room.";

    public const string FreezeFade =
        @"Show panel → FREEZE · FADE · LOOK BACK. FREEZE holds the frame every output shows — the windows, the NDI sends, the stream — until you press again, while the desk, the preview and the monitors keep moving: a wall processor's freeze on the whole rig, for the moment something behind the picture has to change. A blackout still takes a frozen output, and the fade that runs when the freeze lifts starts from the frame the room was seeing. FADE TO BLACK and FADE UP take the seconds you type beside them and land where the picker says: EVERY SCREEN is a blackout with a fade of its own, whatever the transition setting says; THE FOCUSED SCREEN (the wall tile you clicked), THE TICKED SCREENS and THE TICKED GROUPS fade that part of the rig alone while the rest keeps its picture, and FADE UP on EVERY SCREEN brings back every screen that was black on its own. WITH THE SOUND, on by default, fades the music and a clip's soundtrack with a fade that darkens the whole rig and back with the fade up; BLACKOUT covers everything and leaves the sound alone. On the wire: FADE 2 SCREEN 2, FADE GROUP A, FADE FOCUSED, FADE TICKED; a cue's Fade to black / Fade up carry the same places. LOOK BACK puts the look that was on air before the current one back on air; pressing it again swaps the two. On the wire: FREEZE ON / OFF / TOGGLE, FADE 2, FADE UP 2 (or FADEUP 2), LOOKBACK; /patterns/freeze, /patterns/fade 2, /patterns/fade/up 2, /patterns/lookback over OSC; the phone remote's SHOW and LOOKS tabs and Companion's FREEZE, FADE and PREVIOUS LOOK keys. None of it is saved in the show. Machine page → Earlier versions of the show: every save keeps the previous file, and the first save that changes something after five quiet minutes keeps the file as it was, twenty deep; RESTORE puts a version back exactly as Load show would. docs/PRO-FEATURES.md is the study behind these: what the big rigs do, what Patterns already does, what was worth building now and what would cost the stability.";

    public const string BezelsGaps =
        @"Where a wall has no pixels — the bezels between the displays of a video wall, the air between LED pillars — the content should be laid out as if the pixels were there, so a line across the wall is straight in the room and a thing that moves across a gap goes behind it. Screens page → select the screen → Wall gaps: for a canvas of joined displays, Bezel H / V is the dead width between neighbours in pixels (measure the bezel and the air in millimetres, divide by the pixel pitch); for one output whose raster packs panels that stand apart (three LED pillars in one 1152-wide raster, a wall controller's four displays in one 4K), add a gap where each strip stands — before which raster pixel, how wide — or Set from grid for an even wall. Every pattern, video, web page, layer, overlay and lower third spans the gaps and the output leaves them out; the LED wall and video wall patterns put their tiles on the real panels when their raster matches the screen's and draw the gaps black with GAP n px in them (the video wall adds a ring and two diagonals across the wall: round and straight when the compensation is right, stepped when not); the PGM and PVW panes, the multiview tiles and the phone's thumbnail shade the strips; NDI senders of the target carry the whole surface for a processor that maps the gaps itself. A processor that maps the gaps in its own raster needs none of this — describe that wall with the LED wall pattern's Irregular map.";

    public const string Review =
        @"A multiview tile can show the Preview — what the desk is building in EDIT SAFE, beside the program (SETUP → Multiview tiles; a slate while EDIT SAFE is off). REVIEW (the Show panel, the Pattern page's multiview block, the phone remote's SHOW tab, REVIEW ON / OFF / TOGGLE on the wire, /patterns/review over OSC, Companion's REVIEW key) puts the preview over every multiview full-frame with a REVIEW · PREVIEW chip — a screen's own multiview pattern, an NDI send of it, /multiview on the web remote — until you switch it off, so the next look is checked on the monitor wall before the TAKE. The audience's screens never change with it, and it is never saved with the show.";

    public const string Watchdog =
        @"What the watchdog sees: a crash, and a desk that stops answering for 30 seconds — it restarts the app with the show put back, backs off, and gives up on a crash loop. What it cannot see, the Machine page's advice now says: outputs open but drawing nothing for 30 s while moving content is up (the render path stuck while the desk still answers — OUTPUTS OFF and ON, then RESTART APP), and a stream that switched itself off after an encoder error. A supervisor that gives up leaves a note the next start puts on the health line, so a silent log is never the only record. The beacon (Machine page): tick Send on the main machine and it sends one small UDP datagram a second — who it is, what is on air, the cue on standby, the health line — to the backup's address or the whole network; tick Listen on the backup and its health line and super-check say Main machine seen 1 s ago: live · …, MAIN MACHINE SILENT for 6 s — take over? when the main goes quiet, or its watchdog gave up when the supervisor sent its last word. Taking over — OUTPUTS ON on the backup, the switcher to it — stays your call: two machines each deciding to be the main is the one failure worse than one being down. The beacon carries no control and nothing else leaves the machine. A crash or a hang does not take the render windows down with the desk — that is on purpose, the room keeps its picture — but those windows then answer to nobody: nothing can stop them, retarget them or put the next look on them, and OUTPUTS ON would open a second set behind the first. So every start reads who has this show folder's screens (a record beside the settings, beaten once a second by whoever owns them, which is why a desk that has stopped answering stops beating while its windows play on): a record from a process that died is swept up, a record from a process that is still up is asked for the screens — a desk whose UI still answers closes its own windows within a second and says so — and one that never answers is ended, but only once it is provably that same process and provably Patterns. The show then goes back on those screens from the same recovery record a watchdog restart reads, so the picture returns to what the audience was seeing, under this desk. What that record holds is the program itself — the whole picture the audience was watching, brand kit, the lower third on air and where it is in its life, the NDI senders, every locked screen's own picture, and any screen faded to black on its own — together with whether the desk was split (EDIT SAFE open) when it went down. So a restart of any kind, the watchdog's own or RESTART on the Machine page, comes back with the audience's picture on the program and the half-built one still in the preview, where they were: EDIT SAFE as it was, the caller's place as it was, and the look tallies naming what is up. A desk that was editing live comes back editing live. The stream is the one thing a restart does not put back by itself — it pushes to somewhere public, so the desk says the stream was live and leaves the press to you. The health line and Machine → STABILITY name what was found and what was done — Took 2 screens (Main wall, Foyer) back from the last run (pid 4242, ended — it had stopped answering) — and the tick 'Take the screens back from a run that is still playing' turns it off, after which a start leaves them where they are and says so instead. A show folder on a share is never touched: another computer's record is left alone. After a crash restart the health line and STABILITY on the Machine page say what the last run ended in, when and after how long: an access violation (exit code 0xC0000005) is a native fault — a decoder, a driver or a library, not Patterns' own code — and the run after one is a safe run: clips decode in software so one show rules the graphics driver's decoder in or out; Machine → VIDEO DECODING sets Auto, Hardware or Software outright and says what this run does. With createdump.exe beside Patterns.exe the watchdog keeps a mini-dump of the next native crash in the crashes folder beside the settings (the newest three), and the support bundle carries the note and the newest dump — send it rather than describe it. A fault in the desk itself — a page that fails as it comes in, a button whose handler throws, a timer's tick — is contained rather than ending the app: the status line reads A fault was contained and the desk carried on — <type>: <message> — in <where>, the health line counts it (1 fault caught, show kept running …), patterns.log has the stack under UI fault contained, and the outputs, the remotes and the cue stack are untouched. What cannot be contained (out of memory, a native fault) still restarts under the watchdog, and then the health line names what threw and where — The last run ended in an unhandled .NET exception — InvalidOperationException: … in MainWindow.ApplyDeskLayout — at 21:14:02 after 12 min — so send that sentence with the bundle.";

    public const string Osc =
        @"Remote page, OSC: tick OSC in and Patterns listens on UDP port 9698 while remote control is on — QLab's network cues, TouchOSC, a lighting desk with OSC out, Companion's OSC. Every address starts /patterns/ and means exactly the TCP line it maps to (/patterns/look 3, /patterns/blackout 1, /patterns/screen/2 0, /patterns/cue/go, /patterns/lowerthird Neon ""Jane Doe"", /patterns/person 2 …): a number or a name rides as the next address segment or as the first argument, a switch is 1/0, a float above 0.5, a bool or on/off/toggle, a fader's 0.0–1.0 float is a music level, and the journal reads the sender's address. A refused command answers /patterns/error to the sender with the same sentence the TCP port writes; /patterns/ping answers /patterns/pong. Feedback to names a host: every change sends one bundle of /patterns/state/… messages there — live, blackout, the program, the cue on standby and the next, the lower third and its person, each screen's switch and lock, the music — so a fader page or a tally reads the show. The overlays too: /patterns/clock, /patterns/message ""Doors open"", /patterns/countdown 5, /patterns/countdown/to ""19:30"", /patterns/logo, /patterns/pip, /patterns/overlays/off — and the feedback carries /clock, /message/text and /countdown/remaining, every second while a countdown runs. The full list is in docs/REMOTE.md and under the OSC block on the Remote page.";

    public const string PeopleLibrary =
        @"Lower thirds page, LIBRARY: every person (or line of information) the show will name — a name, a role, a company (empty = the brand kit's), a photo and a note for you — kept apart from the designs, so one design serves the whole speaker list. IMPORT LIST reads a CSV or the first sheet of an Excel file by header (Name, or First name and Last name; Role; Company; Photo; Note — Template CSV shows the columns); Append updates a name that is already there instead of doubling it; Export CSV writes the library back. USE puts a person into the selected design — the Name, Role and Company elements read the fields, the photo lands in the picture element named Photo (else the first picture) — and SHOW does that and puts it on air. From a cue: Lower third on, pick the design and the person (blank = as designed); the checks refuse a person who is not in the library, so a wrong name never reaches the screen. The Show panel's PEOPLE chips, the phone remote's PEOPLE buttons, PERSON n and LT design WITH person on the wire and the Companion module's PERSON keys put the person into the design on air (else the last shown, else the first) and show it again — the next speaker in one press. On the page a person's row and card show the name and the role — what the screen draws — and the company, the photo and the note sit under More, a drop-down whose header reads the entry without opening it (More — Acme Ltd · no photo · note); open or closed is your choice and stays as you move through the list.";

    public const string CueSheet =
        @"Every verb the show has is a cue's action — the picker lists them all, the same ones the panel, the wire, OSC and Companion send (the clock's hours, seconds and date, the message's scroll, a countdown to a time and its label, the logo, the PiP, a clean picture, the pattern's kind, freeze, tone, stop all, a look into the preview, the look before, a web page opened…) — but the desk's own keys: TAKE and CUT, GO, review and the admin verbs stay the desk's, and the checks say so if a sheet or the assistant names one. Cues page: IMPORT SHEET reads a CSV or the first sheet of an Excel file into the list in one go — Number, Name, Track, Start (HH:mm), Duration (mm:ss, 5 min), Follow, Mark (break, lunch, end), Confirm, a Look by name, an Action with its Target and Value, Notes — any of them, by header name, in any order; Template CSV gives you the columns with rows to copy, Export CSV writes the list back the same way. Click a cue and its settings — the number, name and track, ready and confirm, the notes, the running order, the quick look and actions, move and remove, FIRE NOW and the ACTIONS block — open in the column beside the page (◀ CLOSE hides it, SETTINGS ▸ brings it back), so the list keeps its room. Each cue carries a planned start and length, a mark and a follow (Running order row), and gets its look in one pick and an action in one press (Quick row). Run surface: the LIVE strip says ON TIME, 3 MIN LATE or 2 MIN EARLY from the last GO against its planned start; the line above the list says when the next break, lunch and the end are expected (≥ when a cue has overrun or has no length); every row shows its planned and expected start; −1 MIN / +1 MIN move every planned start from the standby cue on, RESUME NOW plans the standby cue for now and moves the rest with it, CATCH UP squeezes the planned lengths before the next mark in proportion (never under 30 s) until the lateness is made up. A cue with a Follow fires the next one by itself when the delay is up — through the same GO gate, so HOLD, moving standby or disarming stops it, and STOP FOLLOW on the standby card does too. The standby cue's clip is pre-rolled: the moment a cue with a look that carries a video clip goes on standby, the decoder opens the file, decodes its first frame and holds it there, silent — the Run strip shows PRE-ROLLED beside STANDBY (green), PRE-ROLLING… while it opens, CLIP NOT OPEN when the decoder limit is taken by live sources or the file will not open — so GO lands on the picture instead of on a decoder opening. Move standby on and the held clip goes; a clip already on the screens is never held (CLIP ON AIR); GO still works without a pre-roll, the clip just opens at the press. A cue with a shape in time: every step has an After — seconds to wait after the step above it — and 0, the default, means it goes with the GO. A cue whose steps are all 0 is what it has always been, one edit and one change on the screens; put 3 on the lower third and the picture lands on the GO and the name comes up three seconds later. The row shows where the step falls in the cue (+3 s) and the line under the list says how long the whole cue runs for. Reorder with the grip (⠿) — drag it, or the ↑ ↓ buttons — and the waits stay where they are: the cue keeps the timing you built and only the step moves, so the list order is always the running order. What is still to come is dropped, always, by the next GO on that list (the show has moved on), by STOP ALL, by disarming the list and by resetting it — a step from a cue two cues ago can never land on the audience. The desk's status line names each one as it runs, and the journal has it with the cue's name and its place in it. Sheets carry it too: the After column exports and imports with the rest.";

    public const string Layers =
        @"Every target's pattern carries two layers (the Layers page on the BUILD rail, before the library and the assistant; EDITING TARGET at its top says whose): a still, a clip, an NDI feed, a capture device or another screen's picture, each in a box you place as a share of the canvas, with a fit (Fill crops to cover, Fit letterboxes), a crop from all four sides, rounded corners, a border and an opacity. They draw over the pattern and under the overlays and the lower third, on every span, NDI send and the stream, and they belong to the pattern — so a look, a cue and a TAKE carry them. Anything you see on the PREVIEW pane that can move, you can drag: a layer, the logo, the clock, the countdown, the message and the PiP inset all follow the pointer (the one on top wins where they overlap; a ticker moves up and down only); an empty layer shows a dashed box so it can be placed before its picture exists, and dragging never starts a crossfade — a new picture does. With EDIT SAFE on the drag lands in the preview until you CUT or TAKE; off, it goes straight to air. A drop is told from the nearest anchor: nothing moves — the box is exactly where you let go — but the anchor and the Nudge X / Y sliders come back to counting from the corner, edge or centre the element is actually near, instead of from the anchor it started at. That matters because the nudge is a share of the canvas: a chip dragged to the bottom-right from the middle used to read +42 / +43, which is a displacement, not a place, and it landed hundreds of pixels short of that corner on a wall of another shape and slid as the box's own size changed. Told as “bottom-right, nudge nothing” it stays in the corner on a 32:9 wall and at any size. Under the Nudge sliders, Place X / Y (px) reads where the box's top-left actually is on the canvas the PREVIEW pane is showing, with the box's size and the canvas's under it: type in it to place something exactly — to a rig sheet's numbers — and the nudge follows. Position means that position: picking one from the dropdown puts the element on it and drops the nudge a drag or the sliders had built up — bottom right is the bottom right of the screen, not the bottom right plus the last drag — and RESET TO POSITION beside the pixel fields does the same without changing the position, for a drag that went wrong. The drag, the picker, the sliders and the pixels are four ways of saying one thing, and all four move together. On the Lower thirds page, click an element on the stage to select it and drag it to move it.";

    public const string ScreenRoles =
        @"Not every screen is for the audience. A screen's role is its group on the desk — what it is for. On the Screens page click a tile and the screen's settings — its label and canvas name, enabled and own pattern, the role, follows cues, mirror of, direct output, orientation, frame rate, display mode, output trims, warp and edge blend — open in the column beside the page (◀ CLOSE hides it, SETTINGS ▸ brings it back). Give a screen a role — Main, Confidence (a stage monitor), Info (a foyer screen) or Repeater — and it says what the screen is for; the NDI sends and the stream are feed screens, a group of their own, made on the NDI and Stream pages. Every wall tile reads the group on its foot line (MAIN, CONF, INFO, REP with what it repeats, NDI or STREAM) and a click there opens the Screens page on that screen, so allocating a screen to a group, or moving it, is two clicks from the switcher. What the roles do: CONF and INFO screens are locked as you choose them, so a look, a cue, a clicker step, TAKE ALL or a stinger changes the main screens and leaves them alone; a locked screen keeps exactly the picture it had, as its own pattern, until you SEND the preview to its tile, OWN and edit it, or unlock it. LOCK on every wall tile is the same switch (the tile reads amber, held, while a send is being built), and a cue (Screen lock / unlock), the remote (LOCK n ON / OFF) and Companion can flip it mid-show — 'from cue 12 the comfort monitor stops following'. Mirror of makes a screen a repeater of another screen or a joined canvas: it draws that target's picture wherever the picture goes, has none of its own, and the tile says what it repeats. SEND on a tile puts the preview on that one target alone — every other target stays as it was — beside the tick and SEND TO TICKED for several at once.";

    public const string PartsMultiviewStream =
        @"Playlist parts (Media page; their daily start times also sit on the Looks page): split the playlist into named parts of the show; click ▶ to put one on air, give parts daily start times, or switch with SECTION n from the remote. Multiview (Pattern page → type Multiview): a monitor wall of program/screens/inputs/clock with labels and red on-air tally — each tile at its target's real shape and with the wall's own name for it, so a joined canvas is one wide tile and a screen inside one shows its own half — assign it to any screen or NDI sender, and watch it remotely at the web-remote address + /multiview. Stream page: send one screen to up to two RTMP/SRT/UDP destinations with a single encode (full build, Windows); it never auto-starts, and stream keys live in your settings file, so treat saved shows as sensitive. The encoder runs in a process of its own, fed by the desk and watched the way the watchdog watches the app: a fault in it ends the encoder, not the desk, and the encoder is started again in seconds while the show carries on — the page reads LIVE with the process named, 'Encoder restarting … the show is untouched', or why it stopped; after a crash loop it stands down with the reason and START tries again. The health block on the Stream page — and the light at the foot of the rail on every page — reads the same thing in one word: OFF (nobody asked), UP… (coming up), LIVE (up and taking frames at the rate asked for), SLOW, FAULT or NO DEST. SLOW is the one worth knowing about, because it is the failure the desk's own windows cannot show you: the encoder is up and taking frames at less than four-fifths of the rate asked for, so the wall looks perfect while the online audience watches a slideshow — lower the size, the rate or the bitrate. LIVE* means it is keeping up now but the encoder has restarted since you started it. The block also counts the frames encoded, the rate going in against the rate asked for, and the destinations. Where you press it: the Stream page, the Show panel's SHOW CONTROLS drawer (so a caller never has to leave the page they are on), a cue action (Start stream / Stop stream), a look — which is what gives an F-key, the presenter's clicker list and a permanent install's schedule a stream command, since all three recall a look — the phone's SHOW tab, OSC (/patterns/stream 1|0, with /patterns/state/stream/health coming back), and a Stream Deck. Loading a look into the preview never touches the stream: nothing that has not gone to air may reach the internet, and no restart starts one by itself.";

    public const string BreakMusic =
        @"Break music (Audio page): your Spotify playlists, albums and songs as one-press buttons for the room between the show's own content — walk-in, the interval, the wrap. Patterns drives Spotify rather than decoding it (Spotify's DRM does not allow that): the sound comes out of the Spotify app on the desk machine or any Spotify Connect device, and Patterns tells it what to play, how loud and when to stop, reading back what is on. Needs Spotify Premium and your own free Client ID from the Spotify developer dashboard, with the three 127.0.0.1 redirect addresses the Audio page lists registered on that app; CONNECT signs in through your browser and the sign-in is kept in patterns.spotify.json beside the settings — never inside a show file. It ducks under a sound stinger like the music track, STOP ALL pauses it, and it is a cue action (play, pause, skip, level), a MUSIC remote verb and a Companion key. BROWSE SONGS lists what is inside one of your playlists or a pasted playlist, album or artist link, and Search Spotify finds songs, albums, playlists and artists — add any of them as a button. A look can start one of those buttons, or pause the music, as it goes on air (the Music picker on the Looks page); loading a look into the preview leaves the music alone. Browsing and searching work on any account; starting playback is Premium-only, by Spotify's rule. Switching the feature off is not a stop — pause first — and closing Patterns leaves the music playing on purpose.";

    public const string LowerThirds =
        @"Lower thirds page (BUILD): a design is a box of elements — text that says a name, a role, a company, the date, the time or your own words; bars; a picture; the brand logo; a clip or still; particles; a fractal — each with a fill or gradient, corners, a border, a shadow, a glow and a chaser, and each with its own way in and out (pick a motion chip, or edit the keys: offset, opacity, scale, rotation, a wipe, with an ease). Start from one of ten presets, fill in the fields, scrub or play the preview, and SHOW: it goes on air over whatever is showing, on every screen, NDI send and the stream at once, on the same frame of the master clock, and reacts to effect stings like the full-screen studio. HIDE (or its own hold) takes it off the way it was designed to. Fire from the Show panel, a cue (Lower third on / off), the phone remote and Companion (LT n, LT OFF); a look saved while one is on carries it. Designs live in the show file; Save as a file writes one to the lowerthirds folder to carry to another show. The stage and its timeline stay pinned at the top of the page while the designs, the library and the elements scroll under them, and the selected element's settings — its box, opacity and delay, the words, the font, the colours, the media, the motion and its keys — open in the column beside the page (◀ CLOSE hides it, SETTINGS ▸ brings it back), so an edit shows without scrolling back; the line beside PREVIEW names the design, counts its elements and says ON AIR or IN PREVIEW while it is up.";

    public const string VogStingers =
        @"VOG and stingers (Audio page): one-press sounds and clips. A VOG (voice of God) plays over everything — the music ducks, a stinger that is playing ducks too and carries on, the content carries on, and a clip hands the screens back when it ends. A STINGER is a transition hit — the music fades out fast, the picture dissolves into the clip, and when it lands the show does what you chose: back to what was on, hold for your take, GO the next cue through the caller's gate, or a look or cue you picked; anything that cannot run puts the show back. STOP ALL always puts a held stinger back and never runs its ending. An effect sting (+ Add effect pulse on the Audio page) is a stinger with no file: a shape that runs through the particles and fractals on screen for the length you set and settles back by itself — the four pulses (an explosion, a rush, a flash, a bloom: a rise and a settle) or a scored sting that changes the picture's settings in phases (a shockwave, a vortex, a strobe, a supernova, a freeze, a gust, a rainbow, a quake). It owns nothing and changes no music, so it fires alone, over a clip, or in a cue beside a look. The tally: whatever is playing lights its row on the Audio page and its chip on the Show panel — the seconds on air, HOLDING for a held frame, a bar running down a sting — and the look in use lights on the Looks page and the Show panel: red PROGRAM for the picture on air (PROGRAM · EDITED once it has been changed since), green PREVIEW for the look loaded into the sandboxed preview. A stopped sound or clip fades out over the stop fade (Audio page) rather than cutting, and can never be heard again under the next one. One clock: the pictures, every fade and duck, and every audio output run on the show's master clock — each output device is measured against it and pulled into line (MASTER CLOCK & SYNC on the Audio page shows each output's clock, the correction and the lag; untick the lock and they free-run, measured), so two outputs never drift apart over a long set. Lip-sync: a device row's delay holds that output back (IMAG, a far speaker), Video sound delay moves every clip's soundtrack against its picture, the Stream page's audio delay rides inside the encode. SYNC CHECK flashes every screen, NDI picture and the stream white every two seconds and clicks the computer output at the same instant — count the gap at the far end and set the delay. Live DUCK (D, the SHOW CONTROLS drawer, the phone, Companion, a cue): everything but a VOG drops to the Audio page's live-duck level so someone can speak from the room, and stays down until you lift it — STOP ALL leaves it. Fire either from the Show panel, a cue, the phone remote (VOG / STING keys refuse the other kind) or a Stream Deck. Watchdog (Machine page): the app runs supervised — after a crash or a 30-second UI freeze it restarts itself with the same settings and puts live outputs (and the audio track) back. A clean close never auto-restores. The health line on the Show panel counts uptime, watchdog restarts and contained render faults. STOP always works, session or no session: a clip left on the screens that nothing owns any more goes and the last show that was on comes back (the status line says which), a clip whose decoder is already open plays from the top when pressed again, and a clip that stops moving is put back by itself after fifteen seconds — a stinger can no longer be 'ended' on the desk while its picture stays up. On the Show panel the VOG and STINGER chips sit three to a row, each a name over a small line that says the kind or the after-choice until it plays — then the chip goes red and the line reads the tally: ON AIR · 12 s, HOLDING, SURGING · 0.4 s left.";

    public const string Machine =
        @"The Machine page (ADMIN) watches the machine while you watch the show. RUN SUPER-CHECK checks everything at once — the computer, the graphics cards and which one renders, every display with its mode, the outputs and their frame rate, NDI, the stream, audio, the remote, video playback and the advice, each with a green, amber or red light — says what level of show the hardware is good for, and saves the report beside the exe (Copy report pastes it into an email); the Machine and Help pages widen the desk while they are open. Below it: live CPU/memory/GPU/frame-rate with three-minute history charts, plain-language suggestions when something needs attention (on battery, memory climbing, frames dropping, disk filling), a computer overview with one-click support info, and a Restart app button that brings the show straight back. A rolling patterns.metrics.csv (30-second samples) records the night for after-show reading. Graphics card: Patterns detects and uses the best GPU by default and registers the choice with Windows (so video decode follows); pick Power saving, a specific adapter, or Let Windows decide — changes apply after a restart. The desk's tick: the desk polls every service once a second on the same thread as every click, so the STABILITY block reads what that tick costs (Desk tick 1.2 ms · worst 4.1 ms (tallies) in the last minute), the super-check has a Desk tick row (green under a desk frame of 16 ms, amber past it, red past 50 ms — with the area that took the time), and the RENDER tile carries the worst; an area that fails is carried past, counted, and logged once a minute, so a broken probe never stops the cues, the tallies or the clock. STABILITY also carries the engine's frame budget: every frame the preview, the monitors and the outputs draw is timed with the stage that took it (the pattern, the layers, the overlays, the lower third, a crossfade), and the line names the worst frame of the last minute with its stage and its sink — Render frame worst 31 ms (the lower third) on Output 1 (Main) — so a slow frame says what to lower; the super-check's Render frame row goes amber past 25 ms (a hitch the room can see) and red past 50 ms (a stutter) with the advice for that stage, and the RENDER tile shows the same number. Under it the start-up line: how long this start took to become a desk, phase by phase (the runtime, the settings, the services, the window, the first frame) — past 8 s is a slow start, past 20 s something waited (a network share, a device, a sleeping display), and the longest phase says which. Under that the runtime line: the .NET this build runs on and the garbage collector's mode — sustained low latency while the outputs are live (collections in the background, no full stop-the-world collection for the length of the show), interactive off air, when memory goes back between shows. QUALITY LADDER is a game engine's dynamic quality for the effects: on Auto, when an output's frames run past 25 ms for three seconds in a row, the particles and the fractal iterations step down a level (70%, then 50%, then 35%) on every sink at once — the outputs, the NDI feed and the preview keep the same picture — and step back up after thirty clean seconds; the line says the level, the sink and the seconds that caused it, and how many times it stepped this session. Full never steps down (a slow frame stays slow and the frame budget says so); Balanced and Economy lock one or two levels down for a small laptop from the first minute. MEMORY CEILINGS puts numbers to the app's bounded memory — the working set against a quarter of the machine (512 MB to 3 GB), ten pictures cached, four decoders, decoded frames held 400 ms for a fade — so a working set that climbs past its ceiling reads on the super-check's Memory ceiling row as a leak (restart between sessions) or a jump (a very large picture or deck) before it costs the show.";

    public const string Weather =
        @"The weather chip is an overlay like the clock: the engine draws it on every sink — each screen, an NDI send, the stream — from one forecast the show fetches, so every surface reads the same figure. Three views: Now (this hour's degrees, the sky in words, the wind, the chance of rain), Rest of today (the range, the day's sky, small columns every three hours) and Tomorrow (the range, its sky, morning to evening). The place: search for a town and a country (OpenStreetMap's search, one request per press), pick the result, and the name and the coordinates fill in; a venue the search does not know takes its coordinates typed in; the name on screen can be anything. The source: MET Norway's forecast is free the world over, needs no key, and asks two things — a credit (the chip's small line, on by default; its licence is CC BY 4.0) and to be told who is calling (the contact box, sent in every request). Open-Meteo is the alternative: free for non-commercial use with the same credit, and a paid key for commercial shows (the API key box appears when it is chosen). The forecast is asked for every twenty minutes (ten is the floor; the services update about hourly) and the last one stays on screen through a failed fetch — the Overlays page's status line says what came back and when next. What a look carries: the chip's on/off, its view and its looks; the place, the units and the source are the show's, so a look recalled from another show never moves the venue. Live: the Show panel's drawer (SHOW / HIDE, NOW / TODAY / TOMORROW straight to air), a cue's Weather on, Weather off and Weather — the view, WEATHER ON / OFF / NOW / DAY / TOMORROW on the wire, /patterns/weather over OSC, the phone's SHOW tab, and Companion's weather key that reads the figure and the place. STATE carries weather{on, view, place, text, figure} for any controller.";

    public const string Reactive =
        @"BUILD → Reactive: six curated sound-reactive scenes for a walk-in, an interlude, an awards walk-on or an exhibition stand — Ambient Plasma, Subtle Tunnel, Brand Kaleidoscope, Corporate Pulse, Vortex and Star Warp. Press USE IT so a scene is the editing target's pattern type and the page shows live in the preview; press a chip to land a scene, then shape it with the few sliders under it — speed, depth or warp, symmetry, rotation and brightness. A chip is a starting point: it never touches your colours or your sound settings. Colour comes from the show's brand kit by default (the background, primary, secondary, accent and text, in that order, so the picture sits in the client's own dark), or from two to five colours you write yourself, as the particle and fractal studios take them.

The sound moves a scene and never drives it. Pick This computer or an input under SOUND and set how much: the level lifts the brightness inside a bound, the lows open the rings and hurry the clock, the mids deepen the warp. An input is anything Windows captures from — a microphone, a line, a USB capture card, one channel of an interface — and the Input picker beside it lists what is plugged in now, with Default input at the top for whatever this machine's own input is, which is what a show file that travels between a desk and a laptop wants. A device that is not patched yet is looked for again every few seconds, one unplugged mid-show is picked up when it comes back, and a USB box moved to another socket is matched as the same device; the line under the picker says which input answered. With no sound at all — before the music starts, or on a machine that cannot listen, since the listening is Windows only — every scene still runs on the show clock and looks finished. The stings surge through a scene exactly as they surge through the particles and the fractals: a pulse hurries it, turns it, deepens it and flashes it.

Two paths, one picture. The outputs, the PREVIEW pane and the desk's monitors draw a scene as a single pass on the graphics card. NDI, the stream and the thumbnails have no card to draw on, so they draw the same scene on the CPU at a modest size and scale it up — which is why the QUALITY setting names those sinks and not the outputs: Fast on a small machine, Fine when the stream is what the client watches. The two paths are held to the same picture by a test, so the stream shows the wall.

Whole-screen flashes are limited to three a second on every screen, whatever a scene or a sting asks for. That is the rate the broadcast and web guidance draw the line at, and a beat at 128 BPM taken on the off-beat as well is faster than it — so the limit lives in the engine rather than in the presets, and a flash that comes too soon is dropped rather than shortened. The sound's own hold on the brightness is bounded well under a flash. Nothing on this page can turn that off.";

    public const string Fractals =
        @"BUILD → Fractals is the fractal studio, the same shape as the Particles page: a living picture from pure maths, drawn on the graphics card at any size, designed here and used as a pattern (USE IT makes the Fractal the editing target's pattern type, so the page shows live in the preview; nothing goes to air until TAKE or SEND). SCENES are filed by family — Mandelbrot (classic, Seahorse valley, Elephant valley, Spiral arm, Mini-brot, Triple spiral valley), Julia (swirl, dragon, Douady's rabbit, Dendrite, San Marco, Siegel disk, Galaxy spiral), Burning ship (the ship, The armada, Ship's mast), Newton (triad, coast, lace) and Domain warp (lava, ocean, smoke, aurora, neon) — with your own saved fractal presets under Custom; a scene sets the family, the view, the depth, the motion and the palette, and never the sound settings, which are yours. FAMILY picks the maths (a Julia's c beside it); VIEW is the zoom, the centre, the detail (iterations — the outputs cap it at 256 on the card and the quality ladder lowers it on a machine that falls behind), the motion and the CPU quality for NDI and thumbnails; COLOUR is a palette of two to five colours, or the brand kit's five at a press; SOUND is what the picture listens to — this computer's own sound, or an input: a microphone, a line, a USB capture card, one channel of an interface, or Default input for whatever this machine's own input is — and how much, with the status line naming the input that answered and a device that is not plugged in yet looked for again every few seconds; STINGS adds an effect pulse (Vortex, Supernova, Quake, Freeze, Gust, Slow…) that surges through the picture when fired. The Pattern page keeps only a pointer (OPEN FRACTALS) while Fractal is the pattern, as it does for the particles. The Library files every scene under Fractals by family.";

    public const string Badge =
        @"Where it sits in the workflow: a test card on a wall at an expo, a rig day's grid on every screen, a colour-bar check before doors — Patterns' own pictures, seen by everyone who walks past, and nothing on them said who made them. The Patterns badge does: the app's own mark — the test-card icon (the dark tile, the light grid, the cyan cross of the app's icon), the PATTERNS wordmark and a line under it, on a near-black card with a cyan edge and a magenta rule, in the app's own neon colours — drawn by the engine on every output, NDI send and the stream, and on the desk's tiles, so a test card names its maker wherever it is seen. It is on by default and sits in the middle of the lower third; it travels with looks like every overlay, so a look saved with it off recalls with it off, and an older show or look opens with it on. It goes on every picture the app draws itself — the grids and the ramps, the particles, the fractals and the reactive scenes alike — and stays off what is not the app's to sign: media (a client's video, image, deck or web page) unless 'On media too' is ticked, and the multiview, which is a monitoring picture rather than a picture of the show. BUILD → Branding → PATTERNS BADGE: on or off, the position (nine anchors and a nudge — or drag it on the PREVIEW pane like the clock), the height as a share of the screen, the opacity, media too, the line under the name and its words (the app's own by default; a venue's address or a stand number reads well there). OVERLAYS OFF — the clean picture from the Show panel, a cue, the wire or Companion — takes the badge with the clock, the message, the countdown, the logo, the PiP and the weather chip; the next look recall brings back whatever that look carries. The colours are the app's, never the show's brand kit: the badge names the maker, and the brand kit dresses the show.";

    public const string Assistant =
        @"The Assistant page (BUILD) is an optional helper for building a show, not a control surface: it proposes, you apply, and nothing it says ever goes on air. Tell it the shape of the day and the rig — how many screens and what they are for, whether you want the clock, a logo, a welcome message or the weather, who is speaking, where the breaks fall — and it answers in plain words with proposals: planned screens with a size and a role, looks with their overlays, a cue stack for the day with planned times and notes for the caller, lower thirds from the presets with the person's name and role, the brand's colours, or a whole show plan carrying all of them. Or attach the material — ATTACH…: a running order as a spreadsheet or CSV, a brief or notes, a screenshot or a photo of the rig, a PDF, a Word or PowerPoint file, or a mixture, up to ten per ask — and press ASK with nothing typed: it works out a plan from what it read. Each file becomes a chip with a note (a picture is reduced to what the assistant reads best, a sheet becomes a table of words, a document its words, a PDF goes as it is), nothing is sent until you ASK, and what is inside a file is material to it, never an instruction. Press APPLY on a proposal and it lands exactly as if you had built it by hand — planned screens on the Screens page (each its own screen, a gap from the rig; a wall fed by several outputs is one planned screen of the wall's size, and you join the outputs behind it on the Screens page), looks saved on the Looks page, cues appended to the caller's stack, designs on the Lower thirds page — in one publish, and the status line says what was added and what was skipped (a cue action it named that Patterns does not have, a colour that is not #RRGGBB). A proposal that draws — a pattern, overlays, a look's picture — builds in the PREVIEW and only there: EDIT SAFE opens if it was off, the editing target comes back to Program, and what is on air stays exactly as it is until you press TAKE or CUT. What it cannot do stays yours: it never picks a file, a web address or a device, never adopts a planned screen onto a display, never fires anything. It is fenced three ways: a question about its own instructions, Patterns' internals or any key is stopped on this side and never leaves the machine; the model is told what it is, what it may talk about (Patterns and live-event AV only) and what it must never discuss (how Patterns is built or works inside, which model answers, any credential); and every answer says whether it stayed in scope, so an out-of-scope reply shows as declined with no proposals. What it is shown of your show: the names and counts — screens with their roles and sizes, the program pattern's kind, which overlays are on, the brand's colours, the looks, the cues with their action kinds, the designs, how many tracks, VOGs and break-music entries — and the desk's state at the moment you ask: whether EDIT SAFE is open, what is on air and what is in the preview, whether the outputs are live, which screens are joined into a canvas and what each shows, the cue on standby, the inputs open (by nickname, never an address), the sound playing, the lower third on air; never a file path, a web address, a passcode, a token or a key. It is told to read those states before it proposes — never a screen, a look or a design you already have (it updates by name), and to say when what you ask for is already on air. It needs the internet and your own Anthropic API key, pasted once on the page and kept beside the settings file on this machine (patterns.assistant.json), never inside a show file, so a show sent to another desk carries no key; FORGET removes it. Without a key the page says so and nothing is sent. A cloud model rather than one on the machine, on purpose: the desk's memory and graphics card belong to the render engine and the outputs, and a model good enough to draft a show would compete with them; the fence is easier to keep on a model that never sees the machine. The conversation reads newest first — the latest answer sits under the ask box, lit — and lives for the session (CLEAR starts afresh); it is not saved with the show.";

    public const string PortableFiles =
        @"Settings, presets, brand kits and the log live beside Patterns.exe — copy the folder and everything travels with it. Show files (.patshow.json) capture the whole setup for a gig.";

    public const string Extras =
        @"NDI: install the free NDI runtime (ndi.video) or drop Processing.NDI.Lib.x64.dll beside the exe — then send as many outputs as you need (8-bit BGRX or 10-bit P216) and receive any NDI feed as a media source. Every send owns a screen of its own on the rig (Screens page: FEED SCREENS; the wall): the send shows the program, a mirror of any screen or joined canvas at that target's shape, or its own screen with a look of its own. The stream (Stream page) gets the same — a display is captured off the desktop, its own screen or a canvas is rendered by the engine into the same encode. Video/audio and HDMI/SDI capture devices: the 'full' download bundles libVLC; the lean exe uses an installed 64-bit VLC or a 'libvlc' folder beside it. Web pages open on any screen via installed Edge or Chrome. All optional — the app runs without them.";
}
